@vitek-karas, @MichalStrehovsky - I have some questions on this:

1. I'm a little confused at why this was added in the first place. Why would calling `RunClassConstructor` force that a static constructor be preserved on a Type? If the linker decided that all the code in a `.cctor()` was unnecessary and removed the whole thing, calling `RunClassConstructor` would be just fine right? (just like it is fine to call it on `typeof(ResourceReader)` which doesn't appear to have a `.cctor()`).
2. If this would need to be annotated, how would a developer use `DynamicallyAccessedMembersAttribute` to annotate a Type that is eventually passed into `RuntimeHelpers.RunClassConstructor`?

1. If the .cctor has any side effects, why does the linker think it is safe to remove it?
2. I'm thinking of the scenario like the following: https://github.com/dotnet/wpf/blob/ac9d1b7a6b0ee7c44fd2875a1174b820b3940619/src/Microsoft.DotNet.Wpf/src/Shared/MS/Internal/SecurityHelper.cs#L124-L127

```
    internal static class SecurityHelper
    {
        internal static void RunClassConstructor(Type t)
        {
            System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RuntimeHelpers.RunClassConstructor(t.TypeHandle);
        }
```

How could the developers of the above code annotate the `Type` that is passed in to `SecurityHelper.RunClassConstructor`?

```csharp
class Program
{
    static int X = InitX();

    static int InitX()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Initting X");
        return 42;
    }
    public Program() => Console.WriteLine("In constructor");

    static void Main()
    {
        // Notice we're already running a static method and the cctor didn't trigger yet
        Console.WriteLine("In static method");

        // newobj and still the cctor didn't run
        _ = new Program();

        // Now it runs
        RuntimeHelpers.RunClassConstructor(typeof(Program).TypeHandle);
    }
}
```

The above program prints:

```
In static method
In constructor
Initting X
```

If linker didn't analyze `RunClassConstructor`, the above program would not print "Initting X". That's a difference in behavior between linker/unlinked app and undesirable. In _this specific case_ of the `ResourceManager` class, we would be fine if the call did nothing because we didn't call `RunClassConstructor` for it's side effects: we only called it to make sure that we don't run extra code when we're in the critical path.

`RunClassConstructor` is a reflection-like API, so we just treat is as reflection.
